From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: pieroviano <piero.viano@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] Gtk4: a Label is neither focusable nor selectable

Comparing the sample on both engines showed Test1's status label, "XXX item
analysis in progress...", with an opaque grey box behind its text where the Gtk3
reference has plain background. It was filed as a lost Label.BackColor, and it is
not one: the sample assigns no BackColor to any label, Override.BackColor stays
null unless one is assigned, and the box is one text line tall against a taller
label allocation and only as wide as the glyph run. Its colour, rgb(184,183,186),
is the foreground at 25% over the panel background - Adwaita's unfocused
selection. The reference has the identical artefact on another label, in
Adwaita's focused blue, which is what identified it as a text selection.

Both builds set Selectable on the label. Under Gtk3 that was only a mouse
affordance; Gtk4 makes a selectable label focusable, so the window's initial
focus can land on one, and gtk_label_grab_focus() honours gtk-label-select-on-
focus by selecting the whole caption - which the theme paints as a solid box.

A WinForms Label has ControlStyles.Selectable clear and TabStop false: it never
takes focus and its text can never be selected. Both settings are cleared to
match, which is a deliberate divergence from the vendored Gtk3 build - it costs
the ability to drag-select a caption with the mouse, and takes labels out of a
focus chain that WinForms never put them in. Label.Focus() is left alone: with
Focusable clear, GrabFocus() simply fails and it returns false, as WinForms does.

diff --git a/Gtk.Windows.Forms/GtkSystem/Windows/Forms/GtkControls/ControlBase/LabelBase.cs b/Gtk.Windows.Forms/GtkSystem/Windows/Forms/GtkControls/ControlBase/LabelBase.cs
index 03e0c2bd..598f976f 100644
--- a/Gtk.Windows.Forms/GtkSystem/Windows/Forms/GtkControls/ControlBase/LabelBase.cs
+++ b/Gtk.Windows.Forms/GtkSystem/Windows/Forms/GtkControls/ControlBase/LabelBase.cs
@@ -22,7 +22,7 @@ public sealed class LabelBase : Gtk.Label, IControlGtk
         this.Wrap = true;
         this.Wrap = true;
         this.LineWrapMode = Pango.WrapMode.WordChar;
-        this.Selectable = true;
+        MatchWinFormsSelectionBehaviour(this);
         // Gtk 4: Widget.Drawn is gone and there is no overridable OnSnapshot; custom paint moves to CSS (deferred).
     }
 
@@ -44,10 +44,31 @@ public sealed class LabelBase : Gtk.Label, IControlGtk
         this.Wrap = true;
         this.Wrap = true;
         this.LineWrapMode = Pango.WrapMode.WordChar;
-        this.Selectable = true;
+        MatchWinFormsSelectionBehaviour(this);
         // Gtk4-removed (custom paint -> CSS/DrawFunc, deferred): this.Drawn += LabelBase_Drawn;
     }
 
+    /// <summary>
+    /// Takes a label out of the focus chain and out of the text-selection business, the way WinForms has it.
+    /// </summary>
+    /// <param name="label">
+    /// The label to configure.
+    /// </param>
+    /// <remarks>
+    /// A WinForms <see cref="T:System.Windows.Forms.Label" /> has <c>ControlStyles.Selectable</c> clear and
+    /// <c>TabStop</c> false: it never takes focus and its text can never be selected. Upstream nonetheless
+    /// set <c>Selectable</c>, which under Gtk3 was merely a mouse affordance. Gtk4 makes a selectable label
+    /// focusable, so the window's initial focus could land on one, and
+    /// <c>gtk_label_grab_focus</c> honours <c>gtk-label-select-on-focus</c> by selecting the whole caption -
+    /// which the theme then paints as an opaque selection box behind the text. Clearing both settings is a
+    /// deliberate divergence from the vendored Gtk3 build; it is the WinForms contract that governs here.
+    /// </remarks>
+    private static void MatchWinFormsSelectionBehaviour(Gtk.Label label)
+    {
+        label.Selectable = false;
+        label.Focusable = false;
+    }
+
